From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Niko Lowry <niko@neeks.me>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 19:08:01 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] server: give MLX loads the llama-server memory pre-flight

The scheduler predicts a llama-server load's memory, evicts when it
does not fit beside the loaded models, and books the runner at that
size. An MLX load got none of that: the client was booked at the
manifest's tensor bytes, MemorySize reported whatever the runner held
at the moment, and updateFreeSpace handed the difference to the next
load. The KV cache then grew into memory the ledger had given away.

On an M5 Max with 128 GiB, qwen3.8:27b (nvfp4, 262144 context) was
booked at 16.9 GiB and three other runners were loaded beside it. Its
runner logged a 44.5 GiB peak, reached 78.8 GiB resident, and the
machine kernel-panicked on a watchdog timeout from memory thrash.

mlxrunner now predicts a load from the manifest before the subprocess
exists: the tensor bytes, the attention KV cache for the effective
context (num_ctx capped at the model's length, per layer for hybrid
and sliding-window architectures, from the config.json fields the
architectures share), the fixed state of recurrent layers, and the
8 GiB paged-out prefix cache budget. The scheduler runs the same
evict-before-spawn check as for llama-server, with matching log lines,
and the client books max(live, predicted) so the headroom stays
reserved. For qwen3.8:27b the prediction is 41 GiB.

The runner's compute buffers and MLX's allocator cache are still not
counted, as for llama-server; the 20% headroom covers them.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Ollama-PR: https://github.com/ollama/ollama/pull/19419
